From cdc9902fa63ef95ca63d61f01df069138a8a7c74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:21:33 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] broadband-modem-qmi: hand port-addressed SMS to imsroha

SMS with application port addressing (3GPP TS 23.040 9.2.3.24.4 and
9.2.3.24.5) are meant for an application, not for the user's inbox: the
RCS OTP and configuration requests on port 37273, visual voicemail. When
SMS goes over IMS, imsroha keeps them from ModemManager and emits them
on its PortPduReceived signal, where imsroha-rcs takes the OTP. Where the
carrier has no SMS over IP (SKT), the modem receives them over WMS and
ModemManager, which ignored the port header, exposed them as plain
messages: the OTP showed up in the messaging app and never reached
imsroha-rcs.

Parse the 8-bit (IEI 0x04) and 16-bit (IEI 0x05) application port IEs
in the UDH and keep the destination port in the MMSmsPart (-1 without
one). When a QMI modem with the imsroha backend receives an SMS-DELIVER
part for a port other than WAP push (2948, 2949), from storage at load
time, from an MT message indication or as a transfer-route message,
hand it to imsroha's new TakePortPdu method instead of the SMS list,
as "00" (no SMSC address) followed by the TPDU in hex, the form
PortPduReceived already carries. Once imsroha has taken it, delete the
part from modem storage, since no SMS object will ever do so. If imsroha
is not on the bus or refuses the part, it goes to the SMS list as
before, so nothing is lost.

Each part of a concatenated port-addressed message is handed over on
its own; the application reassembles them.

WAP push, other SMS, and modems without the imsroha backend are handled
exactly as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
